        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=-ln\u20619+7{e}^{x}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln\u20619$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln\u20619+7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln\u20619)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+7\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(7{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =7{e}^{1} \\\\  & =7 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
