        ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln\u20612$ is{{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln\u20612$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln\u20612} & ={(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln\u20612} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-ln\u20612} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
